    "narrative": "Today\u2019s slate for Ghost Ride the WHIP centers on maximizing speed and plate appearances against favorable arms. **Corbin Carroll** (80.88) is our clear-cut standout, buoyed by a prime #2 spot and a matchup against Sasaki. The combination of a 4.08 xERA and Sasaki\u2019s slow delivery gives **Corbin Carroll** a massive ceiling for stolen base potential, a critical factor for our 5x5 Roto standing. **Brice Turang** (68.84) and **Mickey Moniak** (68.23) round out the top tier of starters. **Brice Turang** earns his spot at the top of the order with a green light against Matthews, while **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a substantial +14% platoon edge against Leahy, making him a high-efficiency play despite the elite hold on base stealing attempts by the opposing pitcher.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision was bypassing **Cole Young** (37.27), who fell just shy of our 40.0 efficiency threshold for a start. While his #2 batting order position offered some optimism, the combination of a tough matchup against Rasmussen and the park factor hit pushed him below our required floor. We are forced to remain cautious as we manage our remaining game caps, leaving **Cole Young** on the bench alongside our long-term IL contingent of **James Wood**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, and **Giancarlo Stanton**. While we are currently fielding a strong core, we continue to monitor the status of depth pieces like **Jose Caballero**, **Trevor Story**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Travis Bazzana** to ensure we maintain our edge in the speed categories as the season winds down.\n\nMatchup volatility remains a concern with our lower-tier starters, specifically **Josh Naylor** (46.48) and **Zach McKinstry** (46.79). Both are fighting against unfavorable SP skill adjustments today; **Josh Naylor** faces a significant -19% SP skill penalty against Rasmussen, while **Zach McKinstry** is buried in the #9 hole against Brubaker. These starts are purely volume-based plays to preserve our standing, and we will be watching the weather reports closely for any late-breaking changes that might force us to swap in our bench depth to protect our categorical efficiency.",
